===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*bazaz}}, from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*bʰosós}}.

''Germanic cognates:'' Old Frisian {{m|ofs|bar}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|bar}}, Middle Dutch {{m|dum|bar}}, {{m|dum|baer}} (Dutch {{m|nl|bar}}, {{m|nl|baar}}), Old High German {{m|goh|bar}} (German {{m|de|bar}}), Old Norse {{m|non|berr}} (Swedish {{m|sv|bar}}, Norwegian Nynorsk {{m|nn|berr}}).

''Indo-European cognates:'' Balto-Slavic {{m|ine-bsl-pro|*basas}} (Lithuanian {{m|lt|bãsas}}, Latvian {{m|lv|bass}}, Russian {{m|ru|босой}}, Polish {{m|pl|bosy}}), Albanian {{m|sq|mbath||I wear}}.

